# Universe content · how each kind of world's ground, air and clouds are drawn (the terrain kinds)
# radius-km: its size when its system gives none (Earth 6,371 km; Jupiter 69,911; Neptune 24,622; hot Jupiters puffed up
#   to about 1.4 Jupiter widths; the others typical of their kind). solid: has ground (giants have none). water: seas.
# clouds: has a cloud deck; cloud-km: how high it floats; air-km: how deep the air glows at the edge; air-colour: the air's
#   colour at the edge (Rayleigh blue on Earth-like air, dusty orange on deserts, ash-orange over lava). cloud-colour: the
#   clouds' colour (water clouds white; ash grey over lava; ammonia ice cream on gas giants; methane ice pale blue on ice
#   giants; dark haze on hot Jupiters; dust on deserts); cloud-thickness: how much of the sky they cover, 1 full.
# land-share: more or less land than sea (0.06 Earth-like, about a third land). ground: the land's colours by height (km):
#   "below r g b [tinted]; ...", the last for all higher ground (none given: soil patches, grey rock and snowy peaks).
# molten: glowing cracks and molten lowlands. dunes: long dune ridges. frozen-sea: seas frozen flat. snow-patches: snow
#   lying on the high and patchy ground. tidally-locked: one side always to its star (scorched day, frozen night, a living
#   twilight ring between). polar-ice: ice caps at the poles. dry: no rivers or seas (salt flats, dry river beds).
#   tropical-soil: red laterite in the warm wet tropics. life: can anything live there. volcanoes: many (and all active).
# cloud-shift: more cloud (+) or less (-). day-hours: how long its day is. tides: how strong its tides are (Earth 0.5).
# Giants: bands (how many belts across), band-dark and band-light (belts and zones), great-storm (one great red storm, 1)
#   or storm-colour (its storms, e.g. Neptune's dark ones). river-colour: rivers, or dry river beds.
# rock-colour: steep bare rock. base-k: its temperature from its star alone, before any greenhouse (Earth 255 K).
# Templates: every number may be a range (a..b) and every colour a range (r g b .. r g b); each world of a kind takes its
#   own value in each range from its seed, so no two worlds of a kind are the same, and a seed always gives the same world.
# Where a kind forms (a kind with no forms: is drawn only when something asks for it by name):
#   forms: rocky, giant (past the frost line) or hot-jupiter (the closest orbit of a hot-Jupiter system).
#   orbit-hz: where its orbit lies against the habitable zone's middle (1 = in the middle; the Sun's is 1.175 AU, and it
#   moves out with the star's brightness). orbit-frost: against the frost line (the Sun's 2.7 AU). star-brightness: the
#   star's brightness (the Sun 1; red dwarfs below 0.08). weight: when several kinds fit an orbit, how often this one is chosen.
#   size-km and size-spread: its size, size-km times a value in the spread. moons: how many. dot-colour: its colour as a dot
#   from afar. day-hours: one turn of its spin. label: what its card calls it. makeup (and makeup-dry, for one that
#   formed with no seas): what it is made of, lines split by ;. interest: how much the tour's director wants to visit it.
# rings-chance: how often a world of the kind has rings (1 always: every giant of ours has them; rocky worlds now and then,
#   from a moon torn apart inside its planet's pull); rings-colour: their colour; rings: how many specks make them; rings-radii: from and to, in its radii; rings-gap: a gap in them;
#   rings-shade: banded (bright and banded, like Saturn's) or a number (faint and even, like Uranus's).
# volcano-count: how many great volcanoes (30 usual); seafloor-volcanoes: volcanoes deep under the sea too; rivers: no for none.
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · content/planets.unt

@terrain default
name: An Earth-like world
